@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Dropping a View";
}

<div class="container-fluid">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Dropping a View
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how to permanently remove an existing SQL Server View
            using the DROP VIEW statement.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is DROP VIEW?
            </h2>

            <p>
                The <strong>DROP VIEW</strong> statement is used to
                permanently remove an existing View from a SQL Server
                database.
            </p>

            <p>
                When a View is dropped, the View object and its definition
                are removed from the database.
            </p>

            <p>
                Dropping a View does not delete the underlying table data.
                The tables used by the View remain in the database unless
                they are separately deleted.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                DROP VIEW Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DROP VIEW ViewName;</code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                Syntax Explanation
            </h5>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <strong>DROP VIEW</strong> specifies that a View
                    should be removed.
                </li>

                <li>
                    <strong>ViewName</strong> is the name of the View
                    that needs to be deleted.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SAMPLE VIEW
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example View
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we have a View named
                <strong>EmployeeDetailsView</strong>:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE VIEW EmployeeDetailsView
AS
SELECT
    EmployeeId,
    EmployeeName,
    Department,
    Salary
FROM Employees;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                We can retrieve its data using:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM EmployeeDetailsView;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         DROP VIEW
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Dropping the View
            </h2>

            <p>
                To permanently remove the View, use:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DROP VIEW EmployeeDetailsView;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                After executing this statement, the
                <strong>EmployeeDetailsView</strong> View is removed
                from the database.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         VERIFY VIEW REMOVED
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Verify That the View Was Dropped
            </h2>

            <p>
                We can check the Views available in the current database
                using the system catalog:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    name
FROM sys.views;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                If the View was successfully dropped, its name will no
                longer appear in the list.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         DROP MULTIPLE VIEWS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Dropping Multiple Views
            </h2>

            <p>
                Multiple Views can be removed by executing separate
                DROP VIEW statements.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DROP VIEW EmployeeDetailsView;

DROP VIEW EmployeeSalaryView;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Each statement removes its corresponding View.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         DROP VIEW IF EXISTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Using DROP VIEW IF EXISTS
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server also supports checking whether a View exists
                before dropping it.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DROP VIEW IF EXISTS EmployeeDetailsView;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                This statement drops the View if it exists. If the View
                does not exist, SQL Server does not raise the usual
                missing-object error for the DROP operation.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                DROP VIEW vs DELETE
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>DROP VIEW</th>
                            <th>DELETE</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Removes a View object.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Removes rows from a table.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Removes the View definition.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Does not remove the table itself.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Does not directly delete underlying
                                table data.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Deletes selected table records.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT WARNING
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-danger shadow-sm mb-4">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Important Warning
        </h3>

        <p>
            Dropping a View removes the View object from the database.
            Applications, queries, reports, or stored procedures that
            depend on that View may stop working.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Always verify dependencies before dropping a View in a
            production database.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BEST PRACTICES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Best Practices
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Verify the View name before executing DROP VIEW.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Check whether applications depend on the View.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use <strong>DROP VIEW IF EXISTS</strong> when
                    appropriate.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoid dropping Views directly in production without
                    checking dependencies.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Test database changes in a development environment
                    before applying them to production.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary shadow-sm mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            The DROP VIEW statement permanently removes an existing
            SQL Server View. Dropping a View removes the View object
            and its definition, but it does not directly delete the
            data stored in the underlying tables. Always check
            dependencies before dropping a View.
        </p>

    </div>


